 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per light source per fragment. The cost is dominated by a few dot products, a power function, and vector arithmetic.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per light source per fragment [G]. The cost is dominated by a few dot products, a power function, and vector arithmetic [G].
 *   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ for material and light parameters. No large tables are required for the standard model [S1, S2].
 *   **Performance:** Suitable for real-time rendering on CPUs and GPUs, as it is an inexpensive method for shader-based rendering [S2].
 *   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, computationally inexpensive, visually pleasing for many materials [S2].
 *   **Limitations:** Not physically based (does not account for occlusion or interreflection), specular highlights are view-dependent in a way that can look unrealistic for rough surfaces, no shadowing or ambient occlusion included [S2, S7].
-*   **When to use:** Real-time applications where performance is critical and physical accuracy is not required. Good for stylized rendering or as a baseline lighting model.
-*   **When not to use:** Physically based rendering (PBR) pipelines, scenes requiring accurate energy conservation, or when realistic shadowing and global illumination are needed.
+*   **When to use:** Real-time applications where performance is critical and physical accuracy is not required [G]. Good for stylized rendering or as a baseline lighting model [G].
+*   **When not to use:** Physically based rendering (PBR) pipelines, scenes requiring accurate energy conservation, or when realistic shadowing and global illumination are needed [G].
 
 ## Background
-The Phong shading model was developed by Bui Tuong Phong in 1975 [S2]. It was introduced as an empirical model to approximate the way light reflects off surfaces, specifically focusing on the specular highlight. It became a standard in computer graphics due to its simplicity and visual effectiveness. It is widely used in real-time rendering engines, educational contexts, and as a component in more complex lighting pipelines [S1, S2]. The model is often implemented in fragment shaders in OpenGL pipelines [S2].
